// Lesson 7.5 — Design a Big Cave: Flow & Difficulty
// Design-only day. Inventory of what the student ACTUALLY has: the cave
// tilemaps, the Player with jump/coyote/double jump, the Unit 3 turret, the
// Unit 4 enemy, the Spikes and Coin prefabs (which ship with NO instance in the
// scene and, for Spikes, no collider), the Switch prefab + Lever.cs (real
// working code), the `Cave Exit` SceneSwitch object already in the Platformer
// scene, the ladder they built in 7.3/7.4, and StatManager from 5.3.
// There is NO boss, NO boss door and NO boss sprite anywhere in the project.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>7.5 — Design a Big Cave: Flow &amp; Difficulty</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    You have a set of tools, and it is a smaller set than you think. Be honest about it before
    you design with it: you own tiles, a player who can jump, an enemy, a turret, a spike, a
    coin, a lever, a ladder you built yourself, and one exit back to the hub. That is enough
    for a whole cave. Today: zero code. You learn to read a level like a designer, sketch your
    cave on paper, and only then paint it.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">The Problem: A Cave With No Reason to End</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Right now the Platformer scene is a box with a floor. You can walk in it, and there is
        nothing to do. Before you can design a cave you must know exactly what you can put in
        one — so take inventory. Tick every row honestly; several of these will force you to
        place or build something first.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Where it comes from</th><th>What you must still do</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Cave tiles</strong> (floor, wall, backdrop)</td>
            <td><a href="7.1.html">7.1</a> — the tileset in <code>Assets/Artwork/Platformer/Sliced/</code></td>
            <td>Paint them. The existing cave is only about 20 tiles wide.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>The Player</strong>, with walk, jump, coyote time and a double jump</td>
            <td>Units 1–6, on the <code>Player</code> prefab</td>
            <td>Nothing. This is the one tool that is genuinely finished.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Spikes</strong></td>
            <td>The <code>Spikes</code> prefab, tagged <code>Spikes</code> since <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a></td>
            <td>Place them yourself — <strong>no spikes exist in any scene</strong>, and the prefab still has no collider unless you added one in 2.2.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Coins</strong></td>
            <td>The <code>Coin</code> prefab, from <a href="2.1.html">2.1</a></td>
            <td>Place them. Coins live in the Overworld; the cave has none.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>The enemy</strong> on its patrol / chase / attack state machine</td>
            <td>Unit 4, on the <code>MobileEnemy</code> prefab</td>
            <td>Place instances, and check it actually moves in this scene — it needs whatever your state machine needs to find the player.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>The turret</strong> and its projectile</td>
            <td>Unit 3, on the <code>Turret</code> and <code>Turret_Projectile</code> prefabs</td>
            <td>Place instances. Only a couple exist in the project today — you are the one who decides how many the cave gets.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>The ladder</strong></td>
            <td><a href="7.3.html">7.3</a> and <a href="7.4.html">7.4</a> — built entirely by you</td>
            <td>Rebuild it wherever the cave needs one. Nothing about it ships. It is a zone you place and a strip of tiles you paint.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>The lever and its door</strong></td>
            <td>The <code>Switch</code> prefab plus <code>Lever.cs</code>, from <a href="5.2.html">5.2</a></td>
            <td>Wire the UnityEvent, and build the thing it opens. <code>Lever.cs</code> is real, working, tested code — it is the one piece of this project you can trust completely.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>An exit</strong></td>
            <td>The <code>Cave Exit</code> object, already sitting in the Platformer scene with a <code>SceneSwitch</code> pointing at the Overworld</td>
            <td>Decide whether the cave ends there, or somewhere you build yourself.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        Notice what is not on that list. There is no boss and no boss door in this project — no
        sprite, no prefab, no object in any scene. If you want the cave to end at something
        dramatic, <em>you</em> build that thing, and the cheapest dramatic thing you already own
        is a lever that opens a door. Today you decide what the cave's ending is, and then you
        design everything as a path toward it.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: choose your ending before you sketch.</strong> Read
        all three, pick one, and write down why:
        <ul>
            <li><strong>A lever-operated door.</strong> The <code>Switch</code> prefab sits in the cave; pressing E nearby calls <code>Interact()</code>, which fires a <strong>UnityEvent</strong> you wire in the Inspector. Point that event at a door object and have it do something visible — disable the collider so the player can pass, or move a wall aside. The lever never names the door; the event is the wire between them. This is a real puzzle with real code you already have.</li>
            <li><strong>A collectible target.</strong> Somewhere in the cave, put something the player is obviously trying to reach — a coin cluster, or the <code>Axe Item</code> prefab. The cave ends when they reach it, even if nothing explodes. Cheap, and completely unambiguous.</li>
            <li><strong>The existing exit.</strong> The <code>Cave Exit</code> object returns the player to the Overworld hub. Reaching it ends the cave. The cost of this option is that it is already in the scene, so it is not a discovery — you may want to place a second one somewhere the player has to earn.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <p><strong>1. Flow: the path is the message.</strong> Players move toward open space, light
    and reward, and away from spikes and enemies. A level steers without a single word.</p>
    <pre><code>Good: room → glimpse of the next room through a gap → curiosity pulls
Bad:  room → identical exits left and right, both dark → the player stops,
      and a stopped player reads the room as a wall</code></pre>

    <p><strong>2. Pacing: the beat chart.</strong> A good cave is a rhythm of tension and
    release. Yours should have four to six beats, and it should end at the ending you just chose:</p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Section</th><th>Beat</th><th>What the player feels</th></tr>
        <tr><td>1 Entrance</td><td>quiet</td><td>"I belong here"</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2 First hazard</td><td>tension</td><td>the first jump that can kill you</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3 The climb</td><td>release, then tension</td><td>your ladder, with something watching from above</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4 Rest and reward</td><td>release</td><td>breathing room, and coins on the route you wanted walked</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5 The gauntlet</td><td>peak tension</td><td>everything at once, for a short stretch</td></tr>
        <tr><td>6 The goal</td><td>quiet</td><td>the lever, the door, the thing you built — and the cave lets you finish</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p><strong>3. Difficulty curve, at the scale of rooms.</strong> Each section may introduce
    <em>one</em> new combination. Spikes plus a turret is one new thing. Spikes plus a turret
    plus a ladder plus a moving platform is four, and it is not a level, it is a wall.</p>

    <p><strong>4. Signposting: telegraph everything.</strong> Safe routes look safe. Danger is
    visible before it is threatening — the turret is seen at rest before it fires, exactly as you
    tuned it in <a href="3.4.html">3.4</a>. The goal should be visible from the entrance, or
    glimpsed once on the way.</p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Hard but Fair, or Easy but Random?</h3>
        <ol>
<li>Difficulty and fairness are different axes. Describe a cave that is hard and fair, and a cave that is easy and random. Which one do testers call "good" even when they die repeatedly?</li>
<li>Your spikes do not ship in the cave — you place every one of them. What does that make you responsible for that a designer who inherits a level is not?</li>
<li>A lever door asks the player to find a switch and then find the door again. Which of those two is the puzzle, and how does the layout answer it without a single line of text?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read Function-Level Examples</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        A beat chart is data. Read each of these for what it does in isolation, then work out
        what it has to do with the page you are about to sketch.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Example 1 — A museum's audio-guide running order:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Each stop declares how long the visitor is expected to linger.
stops = [{ "entrance", 45 }, { "armoury", 90 }, { "exit", 20 }];</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 2 — A theme park's queue design notes:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// The ride must be visible from the queue, or the wait feels
// longer than it is. Sightline is a queue-management tool.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 3 — A supermarket's one-way aisle plan:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// The route is decided by the building, not by signage.
// If a shopper doubles back, the plan failed, not the shopper.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 4 — An airport's wayfinding audit (comment only):</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// A traveller who stops walking is a sign that the next step was
// not visible from the last one. Count the stops, not the signs.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 5 — A film editor's reel timings (comment only):</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Tension and release alternate, and the release is timed, not
// left to chance. Nobody edits a film as one long crescendo.</code></pre>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: your cave, in writing, before any paper.</strong>
        Answer these in a notes file. They are the design document, and the sketch is just a
        drawing of the answers.
        <ul>
            <li>What is the ending, exactly — a lever door, a collectible, or an exit? Name the object you will place and the one thing the player must do to reach it.</li>
            <li>How many beats does your cave have, and what is each beat's one-line feel sentence? If you cannot write the sentence, the beat is not a beat.</li>
            <li>Which beat introduces each new combination, and what is the single new thing in it?</li>
            <li>Where is the ladder, and what does the player see from the bottom of it? Remember the ladder is a zone plus a painted strip — what is its height in tiles?</li>
            <li>Where are the spikes? They are yours to place, so name the exact count and where each one is. "Some spikes" is not a design.</li>
            <li>What does the player see from the entrance, and what do they see from the rest beat?</li>
            <li>If the player dies at the gauntlet, how many seconds of progress do they lose? Under 20 is healthy; over 60 is spite.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Design Lab: The Paper Cave</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 1 — the sketch.</strong> Pencil, one page, the whole cave: every beat as a
        box or a room, arrows for the path, notes for threats, and a marked symbol for the goal.
        No detail inside any single screen — flow first. This paper <em>is</em> the design; the
        tilemap is only a transcription of it.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 2 — the camera pass.</strong> Your camera shows a fixed width, so mark on the
        sketch what is visible from each beat. Standing in beat 2, the player should see beat 2's
        challenge <em>and</em> a promise of beat 3 — a glimpse of a ledge, a coin just out of
        reach, a light. Adjust the boxes until every screen shows one challenge and one hint.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 3 — the death audit.</strong> Walk your sketch and ask at every beat: if the
        player dies here, what do they lose? Under twenty seconds of progress is healthy. Over a
        minute is spite, and the repair is either a shorter approach or a checkpoint you build
        yourself — a trigger that stores a position and a respawn that reads it. Decide honestly
        whether your cave earns checkpoints or not.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 4 — the inventory pass.</strong> For every object your sketch demands, write
        where it comes from: a painted tile, a prefab you place, or a thing you must build
        yourself. Anything in the third column is a job you are taking on in
        <a href="7.6.html">7.6</a> — count them before you commit.
    </p>

</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>


    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Account for every tool you have, and name the things that do not exist (no boss, no boss door, no shipped ladders)</li>
            <li>State what your cave's ending is, and what the player must do to reach it</li>
            <li>Explain flow, pacing, difficulty curve and signposting, each in one sentence</li>
            <li>Draw a four-to-six beat sketch with path arrows and threat notes, ending at your goal</li>
            <li>Run a camera-visibility pass and a death audit on your own sketch</li>
            <li>Explain hard-but-fair versus easy-but-random using examples from your own sketch</li>
            <li>Commit and push</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-07/index.html">Unit 7: Level Building &amp; Ladders</a> — the design theory page</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-05/index.html">Unit 5: Levers &amp; Doors</a> — the UnityEvent door you are designing toward</li>
        <li><a href="../data/playtests.csv">Playtest data (CSV)</a> — the numbers that argue about difficulty</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — the components behind every object you will place</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="7.6.html">7.6 — Level Studio I</a>, where the paper becomes tiles.</p>
</div>`;
